---
import { CATEGORIES } from "@helloagents/schema";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { REPO_URL, REQUEST_URL } from "../lib/site";

const agentExample = `---
name: api-designer
description: Designs and reviews REST and GraphQL APIs for consistency,
  naming, versioning and error handling. Use when adding or changing endpoints.
tools: Read, Grep, Glob
category: code-quality
tags: [api, rest, graphql]
author: your-github-handle
---

You are an API designer. When invoked:

1. Read the existing routes and schemas to learn the conventions.
2. ...`;

const skillExample = `---
name: release-notes
description: Drafts customer-facing release notes from merged pull requests.
  Use when preparing a release announcement.
category: docs
tags: [release, docs]
author: your-github-handle
---

# Release notes

1. ...`;
---

<Layout
  title="Submit a sub-agent or skill"
  description="Add a Claude Code sub-agent or skill to helloagents.run with a single pull request. What to write, where to put it, and how it's checked."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">// contribute</p>
      <h1>Submit an entry</h1>
      <p>
        Adding a sub-agent or skill takes one file and one pull request. CI checks the format, a
        maintainer reads the prompt, and once it's merged it's live here and installable from the
        CLI.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>1. Pick the right kind</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sub-agent</strong>: a specialist with its own context window and tools that Claude
        delegates a whole job to, such as a review, an audit or a research task.
        <strong>Skill</strong>: instructions (and optionally scripts or reference files) that Claude
        follows in the main conversation when a task matches, such as a format, a workflow or house
        rules.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Add your file</h2>
      <p>
        Fork{" "}
        <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener">
          the repo
        </a>{" "}
        and create one of these. The
        <code>name</code> in the frontmatter must match the file or folder name: lowercase letters,
        digits and single hyphens, and unique across both kinds.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Kind</th>
            <th>Path</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Sub-agent</td>
            <td>
              <code>registry/agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Skill</td>
            <td>
              <code>registry/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code>, plus any supporting files in the
              same folder
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Sub-agent example</h3>
      <pre><code>{agentExample}</code></pre>
      <h3>Skill example</h3>
      <pre><code>{skillExample}</code></pre>

      <h3>Frontmatter</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Field</th>
            <th>Required</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>name</code>
            </td>
            <td>yes</td>
            <td>Matches the file or folder name. At most 64 characters.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>description</code>
            </td>
            <td>yes</td>
            <td>
              What it does <em>and when to use it</em>. Claude reads this to decide when to invoke
              it, so be specific. At most 1024 characters.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>category</code>
            </td>
            <td>recommended</td>
            <td>
              One of:{" "}
              {CATEGORIES.map((c, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && ", "}
                  <code>{c}</code>
                </>
              ))}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>tags</code>
            </td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td>
              Up to 8 lowercase tags, such as <code>[sql, postgres]</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>author</code>
            </td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td>Your GitHub username.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Claude Code fields</td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td>
              Any field Claude Code supports, such as <code>tools</code> and <code>model</code> for
              sub-agents, or <code>allowed-tools</code> for skills. Unknown fields fail validation
              so typos get caught.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>3. Check it locally (optional)</h2>
      <pre><code>pnpm install
pnpm registry:validate</code></pre>
      <p>
        You'll get a list of every problem with the file and what to change. CI runs the same check
        on your pull request, so you can skip this step.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Open a pull request</h2>
      <p>What makes an entry get merged quickly:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>It does one job well, and you've used it on real work.</li>
        <li>
          The prompt is specific: clear steps, what good output looks like, and what to avoid.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tools are the minimum it needs. A reviewer doesn't need <code>Write</code>.
        </li>
        <li>No secrets, no tracking, no instructions to send data anywhere.</li>
        <li>It doesn't duplicate an existing entry. Improving that one is better.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href={`${REPO_URL}/compare`} rel="noopener">
          Open a pull request
        </a>
      </p>

      <h2>Just have an idea?</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={REQUEST_URL} rel="noopener">
          Request an entry
        </a>{" "}
        and describe the job you want done. Someone may already be working on it.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
